-- title:
--   Inverse of Ogg's modular unit is integral over ℚ[j]
-- statement:
--   Let $\ell$ be a prime. Inside the field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of rational Laurent series (Hahn series over $\mathbb{Q}$ with value group $\mathbb{Z}$), consider the element [`ModularCurve.modularUnitSeries`](def/ModularCurve_ModularUnit.html#L127) $\ell$, defined as the product of [`ModularCurve.deltaSeries`](def/ModularCurve_ModularUnit.html#L107) $= q \cdot \mathrm{dedekindEtaUnitQ}(q)$, the discriminant $q$-expansion, with the inverse of [`ModularCurve.deltaSeriesN`](def/ModularCurve_ModularUnit.html#L119) $\ell$, the series obtained from [`ModularCurve.deltaSeries`](def/ModularCurve_ModularUnit.html#L107) by the substitution $q \mapsto q^{\ell}$; thus it is the formal series $\Delta(q)/\Delta(q^{\ell})$. Consider further the $\mathbb{Q}$-subalgebra $\mathbb{Q}[j]$ of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the single element [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) $= q^{-1}\cdot \mathrm{jNumQ}(q)$, the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant (the integral power series $\mathrm{jNum}$ pushed to $\mathbb{Q}$). The assertion is that the multiplicative inverse $(\mathrm{modularUnitSeries}\ \ell)^{-1} = \Delta(q^{\ell})/\Delta(q)$, taken in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, is integral over this subalgebra: it is a root of some monic polynomial with coefficients in $\mathbb{Q}[\mathrm{jq}]$. No hypothesis beyond the primality of $\ell$ is imposed.
--
--   This is the integrality, over the ring generated by the $j$-expansion, of the inverse of Ogg's modular unit on $X_0(\ell)$, the companion of the corresponding statement for the unit itself; combined, the two say that the unit has divisor supported on the cusps. It is used in the analysis of places of the function field of $X_0(\ell)$, where the order of the unit and of its Fricke transform at a place is compared with the order of $j$.
